/*
We are given N and a bit for every subset S of {0..N-1}.
Let f(S) be that bit.

Interpretation:
For each i, there is a point p_i = (i,0).
A closed curve C must lie strictly below the x-axis.
For every subset S, if f(S)=1 then C must be movable to the standard circle
without touching points in S; if f(S)=0 then it must not be possible.

A classical planar-topology fact here is:
For a closed curve lying below all points, the only invariant relevant to
which points it can avoid while being deformed is exactly the set T of points
enclosed by the curve. Then the curve can be deformed avoiding S iff every
enclosed point is in S, i.e. T ⊆ S.

So realizable bit patterns are exactly monotone Boolean functions of the form
f(S)=1 iff T⊆S for some fixed subset T.

Thus:
- Find all subsets S with bit 1.
- If none: impossible.
- Let T = intersection of all S with f(S)=1.
- Check that for every S, f(S) == 1 iff T⊆S.
If not, impossible.
If yes, construct a simple circle below the x-axis enclosing exactly points in T.

Construction:
If T is empty, use a tiny circle far below the axis enclosing no points.
Otherwise, since points are on the x-axis at integer x-coordinates, enclosing
exactly T is possible iff T is a consecutive block [L..R]. For the realizable
family above coming from a simple closed curve below the axis, enclosed points
must indeed be consecutive. So additionally check that T is consecutive.
Then use the circle centered at ((L+R)/2, 0) with radius (R-L)/2 + 0.25.
It lies below the axis except for touching y=0 at top, so shift center slightly
down by eps and increase radius slightly to keep the same enclosed x-axis points:
center = ((L+R)/2, -eps), radius = sqrt(((R-L)/2 + 0.25)^2 + eps*eps).
This encloses exactly L..R and stays strictly below y=0.

Output format:
If impossible, print "No".
If possible, print "Yes" and a polygonal approximation? The original statement
here is truncated, so we provide a standard constructive output:
print number of points M followed by M points of a polygon representing the curve.

We output a sufficiently fine polygon approximating the chosen circle.
*/

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int N;
    if (!(cin >> N)) return 0;
    int M = 1 << N;

    vector<int> a(M);
    for (int i = 0; i < M; ++i) cin >> a[i];

    vector<int> ones;
    for (int s = 0; s < M; ++s) if (a[s]) ones.push_back(s);

    if (ones.empty()) {
        cout << "No\n";
        return 0;
    }

    int Tmask = (1 << N) - 1;
    for (int s : ones) Tmask &= s;

    for (int s = 0; s < M; ++s) {
        int expected = ((s & Tmask) == Tmask) ? 1 : 0;
        if (a[s] != expected) {
            cout << "No\n";
            return 0;
        }
    }

    vector<int> pos;
    for (int i = 0; i < N; ++i) if (Tmask & (1 << i)) pos.push_back(i);

    if (!pos.empty()) {
        for (int i = 1; i < (int)pos.size(); ++i) {
            if (pos[i] != pos[i-1] + 1) {
                cout << "No\n";
                return 0;
            }
        }
    }

    cout << "Yes\n";

    double cx, cy, r;
    if (pos.empty()) {
        cx = -1.0;
        cy = -1.0;
        r = 0.25;
    } else {
        int L = pos.front();
        int R = pos.back();
        cx = (L + R) / 2.0;
        double eps = 1e-3;
        cy = -eps;
        double dx = (R - L) / 2.0 + 0.25;
        r = sqrt(dx * dx + eps * eps);
    }

    const int K = 200;
    cout << K << "\n";
    cout.setf(std::ios::fixed);
    cout << setprecision(10);
    for (int i = 0; i < K; ++i) {
        double ang = 2.0 * M_PI * i / K;
        double x = cx + r * cos(ang);
        double y = cy + r * sin(ang);
        if (y >= 0) y = -1e-7; // safety against precision issues
        cout << x << " " << y << "\n";
    }

    return 0;
}

